On Tue, 2012-04-24 at 14:13 +0000, Palarz Thomas-DCJ738 wrote:=0A=
> All,=0A=
>=0A=
> I've gotten SEAndroid 4.0.3 running on a Samsung Galaxy Nexus maguro=0A=
> (GSM). I've seen posts about it running on Galaxy Nexus already, but I=0A=
> assume that was the CDMA version toro. Thought I'd my 2 cents and get=0A=
> it going on the GSM handset.=0A=
>=0A=
> I did have to manually(-ish) build the zImage in order for mkbootimg=0A=
> build dependency to be satisfied and didn't see that on the wiki.=0A=
> What's the reasoning for creating a separate project for the omap tuna=0A=
> kernel btw? I assume it's because the Android build system is using a=0A=
> prebuilt kernel for the recovery image and we wanted an SELinux-aware=0A=
> kernel in place of it?=0A=
=0A=
Correct.  We have a slightly modified kernel/omap tree that enables=0A=
SELinux and its dependencies in the kernel config and adds SELinux=0A=
permission checking for the Binder.  Then we have a slightly modified=0A=
device/samsung/tuna tree that uses our kernel rather than the prebuilt=0A=
one, defines HAVE_SELINUX :=3D true in the BoardConfig.mk for the=0A=
userspace build, modifies init.tuna.rc, and adds the sepolicy.* files=0A=
for the tuna-specific policy definitions.=0A=
=0A=
> I haven't successfully turned enforcing on yet, but I have some avc=0A=
> denials. I'll try to run audit2allow tonight. The new SEAndroid=0A=
> Manager app with the avc log file save capability is really nifty ;)=0A=
=0A=
You might want to post the denials first for review.  Often the=0A=
audit2allow output is not what you want; instead you may simply need to=0A=
label some files correctly to get everything working cleanly.=0A=
=0A=
> Has anyone been trying to get SLIDE/CDS working with the SEAndroid=0A=
> policy? My last attempt at it didn't work out because the SEAndroid=0A=
> policy isn't being compiled in the Referency Policy format as far as I=0A=
> can tell, but I haven't spent significant amounts of time on it either=0A=
> to be honest.=0A=
=0A=
I briefly experimented with SLIDE as well (as you note, it doesn't work=0A=
presently) and have asked the SLIDE developers for more information=0A=
about its specific dependencies on refpolicy.  I suspect we would at=0A=
least need to introduce the same kind of inline xml documentation for=0A=
our macros so that they can be recognized by SLIDE, and we might have to=0A=
follow refpolicy's directory layout and naming conventions if we want=0A=
SLIDE to work seamlessly.  Might also need some equivalents to=0A=
refpolicy's build.conf and modules.conf files.=0A=
=0A=
I'm not sure though how critical it is, as the SE Android policy is=0A=
quite small and simple so it isn't clear how much you would gain from an=0A=
IDE.=0A=
=0A=
